  "why": "The statement asks only whether a composite n with φ(n) | n−1 exists. Restrictions on a hypothetical counterexample (prime-factor counts, size bounds, counting bounds, index bounds, Kominers' order bounds) move quantities while the question stays open, so they are scored bound. Pinch's exclusion below 10^28/10^30 is a finite search, so instances. The function-field, number-field, +1, spoof, unitary and Deaconescu papers and the two Lehmer namesakes answer different questions: variant. The withdrawn claims are knocked_down. Sabihi's unwithdrawn whole claim is not_credible: it combines a landmark claim, an author with no located record in the field, ten years without uptake, and later papers still calling the problem open. The close call is Kominers 2026: it is a conditional constraint and could be read as none, but it proves an unconditional-given-ω≥15 lower bound on a quantity attached to any counterexample, the same kind of result as the classical ω bounds, so bound. Its AI disclosure says LLMs assisted with 'coding, computations, and analysis' while 'the problem, methods, and eventual written form are my own'; that names no mathematical step produced by a model, so ai_role none.",
